{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>Here, I have given a thousand silver to your brother.</b> To the one you called 'my brother' — for such was their custom, to give to the bride's relatives, as we find with Eliezer." "<b>Here, it is for you a covering of the eyes.</b> A reproach is this, and it is said only to the people who are with you — since they know that he is your husband, they will not stumble over you." "<b>And all.</b> And with all others you shall be reproved, so as not to say thus anymore, so they will not stumble over you, for I imagined so. And the vav of <b>ונכחת</b> is superfluous, and similarly there are many such cases. And like the Jerusalem Targum: 'Here, I gave a thousand silver selas to your brother; this silver I gave you as a gift in exchange for that you were hidden from the eyes of Avraham your husband — had there been any sign at all, I would not have been able to be reproved in words; Avraham the righteous knew that I did not act wisely with you.' Another interpretation: <b>Here, I have given</b> — if I gave <b>a thousand silver</b> and nothing else, to the one you call 'my brother,' <b>here it is for you</b> — this thing — <b>a covering of the eyes</b> — a thing hidden and covered from the eye, which you could have concealed, and you would be embarrassed by it, for they would say: he gave money to her husband in secret, in order to appease him after doing all his desire with her. But now that I gave him sheep and cattle — a thing known and revealed to the eyes — <b>and all, and you shall be reproved</b>: you shall argue with them to say, 'hands testify that I did not approach you, since I gave him such things, and I returned you to him against my will.' Another interpretation: 'Here, Avraham your brother — <b>he is for you a covering of the eyes</b>' — veiling and covering from the eyes of all the living that you are his wife. <b>And to all who are with you</b> — he commands to do thus. <b>And all, and you shall be reproved</b> — and even to the whole world you would argue, saying 'he is my brother'; if so, I did not sin so much."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {2 "In the first Targum passage, the phrase לית חד סמן כל דהוה הוה לי לית אנא כמיסת is rendered hyper-literally; the Aramaic is elliptic and the logic compressed. כמיסת is taken as 'able to be reproved' (from כסס/מוכיח context) but may carry a nuance of 'able to examine/investigate.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:05:13.100808455Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}